## The result
The test kept only the eighth of the compressed slots (the slots are the compressed vectors the decoder reads) that the decoder pays most attention to once it has seen the question. That is 52 slots per question instead of 431.

| set | slots per question | slots vs plain | judge accuracy ↑ (keep an eighth) | judge accuracy ↑ (plain) | difference (95% CI) | McNemar p | n |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| document questions, seed 42 | 52 vs 431 | .121× | .613 | .610 | +.003 [−.011, +.017] | .78 | 1,500 |

- The cell is clean: 0 unreadable judge verdicts, 1,500 rows. It ran on a node with the same system image as the plain run, so the pairing is valid.
- Keeping a quarter of the slots is already settled at both seeds: no loss.
- At seed 42, even an eighth loses nothing. This counts only as provisional until seed 43 is run.
- We still don't know at what point dropping slots starts to cost accuracy.
- The selection step still reads every slot once, so this saves decoder context, not storage.

## Why I'm blocked
The campaign rules say a tie must be repeated on the second seed. 64 of the 65 allowed runs are used. The 65th was reserved for re-running a failed run, "and nothing else". All four candidates passed, so it is free, but spending it on something else changes your rule.

